# The two Noir wasm modules the Noir Studio web bundle fetches at run time.
#
# WHY A PIN FILE AND NOT `flake.nix`'s `noir` INPUT
#
# `flake.nix` already pins a `noir` (ref `codetracer`, rev ca080a58) and that
# pin is NOT this one. It supplies the NATIVE `nargo` the recorder builds
# against; these are `wasm32-unknown-unknown` builds of two different crates,
# consumed by a browser and by nothing else. Sharing one pin between them would
# couple a desktop toolchain bump to a web deploy in both directions, which is
# worse than two pins that each say what they are for.
#
# THE TWO PINS NOW SIT ON THE SAME BRANCH, which they did not before. The flake
# pin was on `codetracer-temp` — a branch whose last upstream sync is
# `v1.0.0-beta.2` (2025-02-18) and whose tracer writes the retired trace.json
# sidecar triplet — for the structural reason that it was the only branch
# carrying a `flake.nix` that anyone would ship. Measured: 50 of
# `metacraft-labs/noir`'s 63 refs carry one, and all 50 are the 2023-24 legacy
# lineage (`master`, `plonky2`, the traits and closures branches,
# `codetracer-temp`); the modern `codetracer` line has none. That requirement
# is gone — the input is `flake = false` and `nix/packages/default.nix` builds
# nargo itself — so the desktop and the browser now compile with engines from
# one lineage instead of two that were eighteen months and 3,699 commits
# apart.
#
# They are still two pins, and should stay two: this one moves on a web deploy,
# that one moves on a toolchain bump, and neither should drag the other.
#
# WHY THE SIBLING CLONE BELOW IS STILL HERE, and when it can go. `NOIR_REV`
# fd96a7d4 is on `ns11/nargo-test-in-wasm`, which branched BEFORE the workspace
# stopped reaching `codetracer-trace-format` by path, so at that revision the
# clone is still load-bearing. On `codetracer` it no longer is: the two crates
# are git dependencies pinned at exactly the `TRACE_FORMAT_REV` below, which is
# where that revision came from. The next `NOIR_REV` bump onto a descendant of
# `codetracer`@ca080a58 makes the `clone_at "${TRACE_FORMAT_REPO}" ...` step in
# `ci/deploy/build-noir-wasm.sh` redundant — at which point delete it there and
# these two lines here, rather than leaving a clone whose only remaining effect
# is to make the build look like it needs one.
#
# WHAT THE DISAGREEMENT COST WHILE IT LASTED, kept because the gate that now
# watches for it only makes sense next to the failure it was written after.
#
# The lexer's non-ASCII comment check lived at
# `compiler/noirc_frontend/src/lexer/lexer.rs` in the old flake pin `5e98f904`
# and is ABSENT from `codetracer`, because upstream removed it deliberately in
# `93bb72f8` "feat!: allow UTF-8 in comments (#12699)" on 2026-05-18 — after
# `5e98f904`'s branch point and before this one. So a `§` or an em dash in a
# comment compiled in the browser and was refused by the flake's `nargo`:
#
#   Invalid comment character: only ASCII is currently supported.
#
# That shipped. The hello-world template carried two `§` and an em dash in
# `src/tests.nr` and did not build under the native pin for about a day while
# the Test Results pane reported five passing tests. The template was
# transliterated to ASCII as a stopgap; the PIN GAP was the actual cause.
#
# BOTH ARE CLOSED NOW. The native pin is `codetracer`@ca080a58, which contains
# `93bb72f8`, and the template carries its `§` and em dash again — verified by
# COMPILING it, not by reading the lexer: `nargo test` from
# `/nix/store/0yki0k9gzfqg0js2wprmisrfr87sriqi-Noir/bin/nargo` reports
# `5 tests passed` on the template as shipped.
#
# THE GATE STAYS, and it is the reason this paragraph is history rather than a
# deletion. Arm U of `ci/test/noir-template-toolchain.sh` feeds both engines
# the template and asserts they agree on VALIDITY — accepted by both, or
# refused by both. Verdict lists could not express that: a file one engine
# refuses shows up there as "0 versus 7", which arm V then blames on the
# healthy component. The two pins agreeing today is not a reason to stop
# checking; it is what the check is currently observing.
#
# WHY PINNED AT ALL, rather than tracking the branch head. A deploy that
# rebuilds from a moving ref cannot be reproduced, and the provenance string
# the entry document carries — `noir@codetracer <rev> compiler/wasm` — would
# name a commit that had already moved. The revision below is what the page
# tells a user it is running.
#
# TO BUMP: change NOIR_REV, run `bash ci/deploy/build-noir-wasm.sh <out-dir>`,
# and check the two sizes in the log against the ones recorded here.

NOIR_REPO=https://github.com/metacraft-labs/noir
NOIR_REF=ns12/acir-listing-and-nargo-test
# WHAT THIS BUMP BUYS: the CONSTRAINTS pane, for a live visitor.
#
# `src/frontend/ui_js.nim`'s `noirConstraintsSink` has a branch for an empty
# listing whose comment reads "a compiler module older than
# `VfsResponse.acir_listing` answers without one — which is every build on the
# current deploy pin." That was this pin. The pane rendered a real opcode
# listing in a browser at 2bf98864 and no visitor could see one, because the
# module this file names has no such field: the deploy took the
# `noteListingUnavailable` branch every time and captioned the counts instead.
#
# `VfsResponse.acir_listing` is `nargo compile --print-acir`'s text, emitted
# through the compiler's own `Display`, so what a user reads in the tab is
# byte-for-byte what the toolchain prints locally. `reportFromAcirListing`
# builds the whole report from it — rows AND counts from one reading of one
# compile — which is why the pane's headline and its listing can no longer
# disagree.
#
# THIS BUMP IS NOT A FAST-FORWARD, and that is the only interesting thing
# about it. Every previous entry below could say "THIS REVISION CONTAINS THE
# ONE IT REPLACES". This one cannot. `acir_listing` lived on
# `ns4/acir-listing`, which branched from `9d4e40a6` — the same commit
# `ns11/nargo-test-in-wasm` branched from. The two lines diverged there:
#
#   9d4e40a6 ── 082333c3 ── 6c1af2c9        ns4/acir-listing
#            └─ df2d2f2e ── 9c2dfc24 ── fd96a7d4   ns11/nargo-test-in-wasm
#
# So moving the pin to `6c1af2c9` — the one-line change this needed to be, and
# the change it looked like — would have SILENTLY REVERTED the three commits
# the line already carried: `nv_test_vfs` (the browser's test runner), its
# `record` path, and df2d2f2e's tracer frame fix. Every note below describing
# those would have stayed in this file, describing a module that no longer had
# them.
#
# `ns12/acir-listing-and-nargo-test` is the merge, and its merge commit
# (8124a763) records the one conflict — `Cargo.lock`, one dependency added by
# each side — and why `compiler/wasm/src/compile_vfs.rs` auto-merging was
# checked rather than trusted.
#
# VERIFIED BY WHAT IT PRESERVES, not by building. A build proves neither of
# the two things a naive bump would have lost, so both were exercised through
# the ABI on the module built from this revision, not read off the source:
#
#   * `nv_test_vfs` is exported (`nv_alloc, nv_compile_vfs, nv_free,
#     nv_result_len, nv_test_vfs`) and ANSWERS: driven over the real
#     `(ptr, len)` protocol on the bundled template it returns five verdicts,
#     all `pass`, and they are the same five selectors
#     `ci/test/noir_template_fixture.nim` reports the shared parser finding.
#
#   * The tracer frame fix is still in `MemorySink::start`: the `<toplevel>`
#     `Call` is pushed before the entry `Step`, and
#     `tests/trace_artifact.rs::the_entry_call_precedes_the_entry_step` — the
#     test written to hold it there — is present and green.
#
#   * The opcode agreement is unmoved at 17. `noirTemplateNargoInfoJson` ships
#     `"opcodes":17`; `ci/test/noir-template-acir-count.mjs` measures 17 from
#     this module's `acir_locations`; and the new `acir_listing` prints 17
#     constrained rows. Three readings, three code paths, one number.
#
#   * `acir_listing` itself is PRESENT and is the listing, not an empty
#     string: 1546 characters over 42 lines, opening `func 0`, then
#     `private parameters: [w0]`, and carrying
#     `unconstrained func 0: directive_invert` and
#     `unconstrained func 1: directive_integer_quotient` — the two names, and
#     the two counts, `nargo info` reports for this template.
#
# THE STRING THAT SAYS WHICH PATH PAINTED THE PANE is `func 0`. The listing
# heads its constrained block that way; the compile-time `nargo info` constant
# names the same function `main`. So a Constraints pane showing `main` is
# reading the shipped constant and a pane showing `func 0` is reading a
# listing this compile produced — which makes one DOM string the whole answer
# to "did the pin bump reach the user".
#
# WHAT THE PREVIOUS BUMP BOUGHT, kept because this file is the only place the
# line's history is written down: `nargo test` in the browser.
#
# `compiler/wasm` could compile a virtual filesystem and could not test one, so
# the Test Results pane listed the tests `nargo test` would run beside a
# paragraph saying no browser could run them. `compiler/wasm/src/test_vfs.rs`
# adds `nv_test_vfs`, which discovers a package's `#[test]` functions with the
# frontend's own `get_all_test_functions_in_crate_matching` and runs each
# through `nargo::ops::run_test` — so `#[test(should_fail)]`'s inversion (an
# assertion failure is a PASS) and `should_fail_with`'s substring match are
# nargo's verdicts rather than a second implementation of them.
#
# AND IT RECORDS. `TestVfsRequest.record` names one test and answers a
# `ProgramArtifact` with that test as its `main`, compiled through the
# instrumented `force_brillig` path — the only shape `ct_trace` can step. That
# is what makes "run this test" mean "land in a time-travel session on it"
# rather than "see a verdict". MEASURED through the worker protocol: a recorded
# test traces to 8 events, 2 steps and 2 calls, against the one event and zero
# steps an uninstrumented compile produces while every module reports ok.
#
# PUSHED, AND CHECKED THE WAY THE BUILDER RESOLVES IT. `build-noir-wasm.sh`
# runs `git fetch --depth 1 origin <rev>` into a directory it created, so the
# check is the same operation, and it was run into an empty clone that had
# never seen this machine:
#
#   git init && git remote add origin https://github.com/metacraft-labs/noir
#   git fetch --depth=1 origin 6eb7d0242ba34d184aba64ab33b8e11a3a2ce406
#
# resolves, and at the fetched commit `compiler/wasm/src/compile_vfs.rs` names
# `acir_listing` in eleven places, `compiler/wasm/src/test_vfs.rs` is its full
# 970 lines, and `tooling/tracer_wasm/src/memory_sink.rs` pushes the `Call`
# before the `Step` — the merge's two survivors and its one addition, checked
# in the tree the builder will actually compile rather than in a worktree.
#
# THAT CLONE IS NOW 24 MB, and it was about 900 MB. `fd96a7d4` carried
# `target-wasm/` — 3,134 files of cargo build cache, including a stale
# `noir_wasm.wasm` with exactly the name of the artifact this script produces
# — because `.gitignore` matched `/target` and `**/target` and nothing else.
# `--depth 1` pays for the whole tree at one commit, so every deploy on this
# line was downloading it. The noir-side commit 6eb7d024 untracks it and adds
# `/target-wasm` beside `/target`.
#
# The previous bump's note, kept because its claim still holds:
# a browser-produced trace opens on `main` instead of on
# a frame the engine invented.
#
# `MemorySink::start` pushed the entry `Step` and then the `<toplevel>` `Call`.
# CodeTracer's `TraceProcessor` opens a frame when it reads a `Call` and
# attributes each following `Step` to whichever frame is open, so a `Step` that
# arrives with none open makes it synthesize a `<top-level>` frame — which then
# shadows both the recording's `<toplevel>` and the program's `main`. Measured
# through the real `replay-server` over DAP on `a_1_mul`: three frames with
# `main` nowhere in them, against `main` on top of `<toplevel>` once the two
# pushes are swapped. Every browser trace this deployment can produce was
# affected, and no native recording was.
#
# THIS REVISION CONTAINS THE ONE IT REPLACES. It is a direct child of
# 9d4e40a6, the contract-debug bump already merged here, so this is a
# fast-forward of the same line rather than a second pin racing it — the two
# do not have to be reconciled because one contains the other.
#
# PUSHED, AND CHECKED THE WAY THE BUILDER RESOLVES IT. `build-noir-wasm.sh`
# runs `git fetch --depth 1 origin <rev>` into a directory it created, so the
# check is the same operation:
#
#   git init && git remote add origin https://github.com/metacraft-labs/noir
#   git fetch --depth=1 origin df2d2f2e48b12ad4628ac75f8be7f252d80fd5c2
#
# resolves in an empty clone that had never seen this machine, and
# `tooling/tracer_wasm/src/memory_sink.rs` at the fetched commit pushes the
# `Call` before the `Step`.
NOIR_REV=6eb7d0242ba34d184aba64ab33b8e11a3a2ce406

# AT `NOIR_REV` ABOVE, and no longer on `codetracer` — see the note at the top
# of this file. The noir workspace at `ns12/acir-listing-and-nargo-test` still
# resolves `codetracer_trace_types` and `codetracer_trace_writer_nim` through
# PATH dependencies on a sibling checkout (`../codetracer-trace-format`).
# Without it `cargo build` fails before compiling anything, with an error
# naming a missing Cargo.toml rather than a missing repository — so the sibling
# is pinned here too. On `codetracer` those are git dependencies at exactly
# this revision, so a `NOIR_REV` descended from `codetracer`@ca080a58 will not
# need it.
#
# STILL LOAD-BEARING AT THIS BUMP, and that was checked rather than assumed:
# `git merge-base --is-ancestor ca080a58 6eb7d024` says NO. Both parents of
# the merge branch from `9d4e40a6`, which predates the switch to git
# dependencies, so merging them cannot have crossed it. `Cargo.toml:173` at
# this revision still reads `path = "../codetracer-trace-format/..."`. The
# `clone_at "${TRACE_FORMAT_REPO}"` step in `ci/deploy/build-noir-wasm.sh`
# therefore stays; the note at the top of this file says when it can go, and
# this is not that bump.
TRACE_FORMAT_REPO=https://github.com/metacraft-labs/codetracer-trace-format
TRACE_FORMAT_REV=235e377ec66b7d140e9c75828e805d53945824e3

# Measured at the revisions above (2026-09-01, aarch64-darwin). This bump
# touches `tooling/tracer_wasm` and nothing else, so the TRACER figure is the
# measured one this time and the COMPILER's is carried forward unmeasured —
# the mirror image of the previous bump, and recorded as such rather than
# leaving both looking equally fresh. The tracer built at 4623865 from
# `/Users/zahary/m/dev/.agent-wt/ns10/noir`; the +5401 against the figure it
# replaces is dominated by the build-directory path length described below,
# not by the two swapped pushes.
#
# ADVISORY, AND THE BUILD SCRIPT ONLY REPORTS ON THEM. These are not
# reproducible byte-for-byte and that was measured, not assumed: building the
# same revision twice from two different checkout DIRECTORIES gave 15862494 vs
# 15862541 and 4618464 vs 4619415. rustc embeds source paths, so the length of
# the build directory reaches the artifact. A rustc bump moves them further.
#
# So an exact-match assertion here would fail every deploy for reasons that
# have nothing to do with the product, and the first person to meet that would
# delete the check. What it is good for is ORDER OF MAGNITUDE: a module that
# arrives at 4 KB is a build that emitted an error page, and that shows up
# against these numbers instantly.
#
# The check that actually protects a visitor is elsewhere and is exact:
# `ci/test/web_deploy_guard.nim` compares the size the entry document DECLARES
# against the file in the publish directory, and both come from the same
# assembly run — so it catches a stale document over fresh bytes, which is the
# failure that reached production in the sibling campaign.
# MEASURED at the revision above (2026-09-03, aarch64-darwin), both of them,
# by `bash ci/deploy/build-noir-wasm.sh` inside the dev shell — so these two
# are the first figures in this file that carry the TOOLCHAIN line below
# rather than a guess about which compiler produced them.
#
#   noir_wasm.wasm         15362193  (-1092496 against the figure it replaces)
#   noir_tracer_wasm.wasm   4359466  (-253691, and this crate is UNCHANGED by
#                                     the merge — nothing on `ns4` touches
#                                     `tooling/tracer_wasm`)
#
# BOTH WENT DOWN, AND THE COMPILER WENT DOWN WHILE GAINING CODE. That reads
# like a truncated build, so it was run down rather than recorded: it is the
# RUSTC BUMP this file predicted. The note above says "a rustc bump moves them
# further", and this is the first bump measured across one.
#
# The evidence is a controlled pair, not an inference from the direction of a
# number. `tooling/tracer_wasm` is byte-identical in source between the
# revision this replaces and this one, so it is a control: built from THIS
# tree at rustc 1.89.0 it comes out 4614241 — within 1084 bytes of the 4613157
# recorded above, which is the build-directory noise this file already
# documents — and at the dev shell's rustc 1.96.0 it comes out 4359466. Same
# source, same machine, same day; the -5.5% is the compiler and nothing else.
#
# SO `MEASURED_TOOLCHAIN=unknown-predates-the-stamp` IS NOW ANSWERABLE, and
# the answer is worth writing down because the field below is about to stop
# saying it. Whatever produced 4613157 behaved like rustc 1.89.0 — the version
# `rust-toolchain.toml` in the noir tree pins, which is what an ambient
# `rustup` honours and what a build run outside the dev shell would therefore
# have used. That is evidence, not a record, and it is stated as evidence.
#
# The compiler's -6.6% is the same bump, working against the ACIR listing code
# this revision adds. The listing's own cost is visible only in the other
# direction: built at 1.89.0 from this tree the compiler is 16487480 against
# the 16454689 recorded above, so `acir_listing` costs about 32 KB — 0.2%, and
# nothing like the 1.1 MB the headline delta shows.
EXPECT_COMPILER_BYTES=15362193
EXPECT_TRACER_BYTES=4359466

# WHICH COMPILER PRODUCED THE TWO FIGURES ABOVE. Recorded, for the first time,
# and copied verbatim from the run that produced them rather than assembled by
# hand afterwards.
#
# The line below is `ci/deploy/build-noir-wasm.sh`'s own output. It printed it
# beside each byte count and again at the end of its log, having first refused
# to build at all until `scripts/toolchain-pins.sh --require rustc cargo`
# passed — so there is no path to these numbers that skips the attribution.
# The previous value of this field was `unknown-predates-the-stamp`, and the
# note that replaced it is directly above: the figures it described behaved
# like rustc 1.89.0, which is one version short of what the dev shell now
# supplies, and that one version is most of the movement in both numbers.
#
# WHAT IT DOES AND DOES NOT VOUCH FOR. Two tools, `rustc` and `cargo`, which
# are the only ones a `wasm32-unknown-unknown` cargo build invokes. `nim` and
# `nargo` appear under `not-verified:` because this run never ran them; the
# stamp keeps them visible rather than dropping them, so the scope of the
# claim is legible from the claim itself.
MEASURED_TOOLCHAIN='rustc=1.96.0[sih46ajs0xm4bch0r0gdiymfa79gi9ri-rust-mixed] cargo=1.96.0[sih46ajs0xm4bch0r0gdiymfa79gi9ri-rust-mixed] | lock: toolchains=942c995a fenix=dd2c80d0 noir=ca080a58 | host arm64-darwin | not-verified: nim=2.2.8[cwcwjh77y174bwqzbffl6bd7y42c6588-nim-2.2.8] nargo=1.0.0-beta.26@906af2f4[worktree noir@61960c8e]'
#
# THE NEXT BUMP KEEPS DOING THIS. Copy the `TOOLCHAIN: …` line the build script
# prints, alongside the new figures — it is the sentence that makes "the number
# moved" answerable, and this bump is the first one that could answer it.
